\fs28 ##Ancient & Early Draped Origins (c. 1500 BCE \'96 300 CE)
\fs24 \
\
\
Even though the modern trapeze skirt appears much later, its roots lie in early draped garments that allowed fabric to flare freely away from the body.\
\
Where: Ancient Egypt, Greece, and Rome\
\
Characteristics: Tunics and wrapped garments fell in a conical or bell shape, creating natural trapezoidal movement when tied at the waist.\
\
Fabric: Linen (Egypt), wool/linen blends (Greece & Rome)\
\
Technique: Voluminous draping created flare without rigid tailoring; garments widened toward the hem much like a primitive trapeze line.\
\
\

\fs28 ##Post\'96World War II Era (Late 1940s \'96 1950s)
\fs24 \
\
\
After WWII, women embraced full, floating skirts with cinched waists and romantic prints (Dior\'92s New Look, 1947). Skirts emphasized femininity and glamour, but by the late 1950s, women wanted modern, minimal, easy-to-wear silhouettes.\
\
1958: Yves Saint Laurent \'96 THE BIRTH OF THE MODERN TRAPEZE LINE\
\
This is the true origin of the modern trapeze skirt and trapeze silhouette.\
\
For his first collection, Yves Saint Laurent set himself apart from the master.\
\
Instead of Dior\'92s signature cinched waist, he created a more fluid silhouette under which the body disappeared.\
\
The Trapeze Line was born.\
\
Saint Laurent made his mark with less fabric and a lighter approach that changed the course of fashion. It was a success.\
Revolution: The body was no longer sculpted by corsetry; instead, the garment created the architecture.\
\
\'95 Narrow at the top, flaring gradually toward the hem\
\'95A-line geometry that inspired the trapeze skirt\
\'95 Soft, flowing volume allowing comfort and freedom of movement\
\'95 Aligned with women\'92s liberation ideals\
\
Note: YSL\'92s 1958 Trapeze Line was a dress, not a skirt, but it defined the shape that later became the trapeze skirt.\
\
\

