\fs72 \cf0 #Iconic trapeze skirt\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 ##Yves Saint Laurent \'96 The Original \'93Trapeze Line\'94 (1958)
\fs24 \
\
House: Christian Dior\
\
Collection: Dior Spring/Summer 1958 \'96 \'93The Trapeze Line\'94\
\
Designer: Yves Saint Laurent (his debut collection after Dior\'92s death)\
\
Although the 1958 Trapeze Line by Yves Saint Laurent is an iconic dress rather than a skirt, it represents the birth of the modern A-line silhouette. This silhouette directly inspired the development of the trapeze skirt in the 1960s. This is the birth of the trapeze silhouette in modern fashion. YSL eliminated Dior\'92s famous cinched waist and introduced a silhouette that expanded outward from the shoulders, creating an A-shaped, flared volume \'97 the same geometry that later defined the trapeze skirt. It liberated the female body from corseted waists and shifted fashion toward fluidity and youthfulness.\
\

\fs28 ##Andr\'e9 Courr\'e8ges \'96 Space Age A-Line Trapeze Skirts (Early 1960s\'961965)
\fs24 \
\
Designer: Andr\'e9 Courr\'e8ges\
\
Era: 1964\'961965 \'93Space Age\'94 collections\
\
Location: Paris\
\
Courr\'e8ges took the A-line trapeze shape and turned it into a fashion symbol of the 1960s modernist revolution. His trapeze skirts defined the Space Age look: futuristic, geometric, architectural. He made the trapeze skirt young, short, playful, and extremely influential on global fashion.\
\
\
